{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 26, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering move to decriminalize personal drug use is ending this weekend, replaced by a new statute that reclassifies possession of substances like fentanyl, heroin, and meth as a misdemeanor. Under HB4002, individuals caught with small amounts may avoid jail by entering a behavioral health initiative and engaging in a diversion pathway designed to steer them away from prosecution. However, failure to qualify could result in up to half a year behind bars, signaling a sharp shift from the state’s previous approach.\n\n***\n\nThe updated law empowers law enforcement to act more decisively in public spaces, particularly near areas like playgrounds and recreational zones, where selling drugs will carry stiffer consequences. While counties are encouraged to set up health-focused alternatives, participation is optional, leading to concerns about inconsistent implementation across regions. Police are instructed to offer a brief window for intervention—if a trained health team can arrive within half an hour, the person may be connected to care. Otherwise, arrest becomes the default.\n\n***\n\nPortland’s police chief emphasized that eligibility for non-custodial options is tightly restricted: no prior offenses, outstanding warrants, signs of aggression, or medical instability. If these conditions aren’t met, officers must proceed with detention. The system relies on rapid coordination between patrol units and mobile health responders, leaving little room for delay or discretion on the street.\n\n***\n\nThis transition reflects a broader national reckoning over how to respond to substance use. While some see flexibility as pragmatic, others worry about fairness and access. In a country where access to care varies widely, outcomes may hinge more on zip code than need. Imagine a teenager who just turned 18, trying to eat lunch after shopping, wondering if their tongue will betray them with cravings they can’t control.\n\nP.P.S Age, country, eat, shopping, tongue", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.00015507926582358778}, {"token": "’s", "logprob": -0.005780527368187904}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.13278159499168396}, {"token": " move", "logprob": -0.10517826676368713}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -8.964136941358447e-05}, {"token": " dec", "logprob": -0.21991808712482452}, {"token": "riminal", "logprob": -0.004677188582718372}, {"token": "ize", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " personal", "logprob": -0.554117739200592}, {"token": " drug", "logprob": -8.904537389753386e-05}, {"token": " use", "logprob": -0.31348296999931335}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.007826023735105991}, {"token": " ending", "logprob": -0.24009528756141663}, {"token": " this", "logprob": -1.1624716520309448}, {"token": " weekend", "logprob": -0.02635258063673973}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.1923881471157074}, {"token": " replaced", "logprob": -0.07059750705957413}, {"token": " by", "logprob": -2.312633478140924e-05}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.019187556579709053}, {"token": " new", "logprob": -0.1946151703596115}, {"token": " statute", "logprob": -0.24270229041576385}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.00016985881666187197}, {"token": " re", "logprob": -0.4679598808288574}, {"token": "class", "logprob": -0.016653569415211678}, {"token": "ifies", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " possession", "logprob": -0.5159779787063599}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.01251133717596531}, {"token": " substances", "logprob": -0.2891737222671509}, {"token": " like", "logprob": -0.00011002412065863609}, {"token": " f", "logprob": -0.00433386629447341}, {"token": "entanyl", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.10020841658115387}, {"token": " heroin", "logprob": -0.00012635385792236775}, {"token": ",", "logprob"